# Results

NASA's SPHEREx telescope maps the whole sky in 102 infrared wavelength bands between 0.75 and 5 µm, photographing each position many times over a year. Epoch reads the [[meta.n_frames]] pictures of the first data release (taken [[meta.mjd_min|date]] to [[meta.mjd_max|date]]) directly from NASA's public archive and shows any position in time order. The results below use the same pictures.

## Removing false changes {#which-change-is-real}

SPHEREx takes each picture at a different wavelength with one of six detectors, so a plain blink between consecutive pictures shows many changes where the sky has not changed. Comparing each picture with the previous picture from the same detector removes a median [[headline.number]]% of these changes (95% interval [[headline.err_low]]–[[headline.err_high]]%) across [[e2.summary.ratio_corr_over_naive_all.n_fields]] randomly chosen patches ([[e2.total_frames]] pictures). This removes false changes but does not make faint real changes easier to detect: planted sources at 20 times the single-picture noise are recovered at similar rates by one detector at a time and by the plain blink ([[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M2.efficiency.4|pct]] and [[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M1.efficiency.4|pct]]), at a shared threshold where the plain blink also flags [[e2_d1.counts.M1.total|int]] false changes against [[e2_d1.counts.M2.total|int]]. For finding faint new sources, comparing each picture with the median of all the other pictures from the same detector works better: on real asteroids in [[e34s.n_ast_fields]] new fields, at the same false-alarm rate, it marks [[e34s.ast_M3BK_hits_V17p5]] of [[e34s.ast_n_V17p5]] sightings, against [[e34s.ast_M2K_hits_V17p5]] for one detector at a time. Sources planted into real pictures show the same order. The change maps also allow every source an uncertainty of [[f1.f|pct]] of its own brightness, so they flag a bright star only if it changes by several times that; smaller variations of bright stars are measured from their brightness instead (see the north ecliptic pole and R Draconis below).

[Methods, section 2](methods.html#change)

