## WISE 0855−0714 {#e4}

WISE 0855−0714, the coldest known brown dwarf, is barely above the noise in a single picture. Combining the [[e4.passes.1.n_frames]] pictures from the November–December 2025 visits along its predicted path detects it at [[e4bs.pass2_sig_200|f1]]σ: its signal exceeds that of the same pictures combined along [[e4bs.pass2_null200_n]] empty paths by about [[e4bs.pass2_sig_200|f0]] times their scatter, and none of the paths is as bright. Its flux density at 4.4–5.0 µm is [[e4.passes.1.a_hat_mJy|f2]] ± [[deriveds.wise0855_flux_err_mJy|f2]] mJy (the error calibrated on the empty paths), consistent with Spitzer's [[e4.expected_flux_mJy.IRAC_4p5|f2]] mJy at 4.5 µm (Luhman & Esplin 2016) and within the 0.41–0.50 mJy spanned by published WISE measurements. Over the [[e11.targets.wise0855.jwst.comparison.PRISM.n_bins_detected]] wavelength steps where it is detected, the SPHEREx spectrum is [[e11.targets.wise0855.jwst.comparison.PRISM.mean_ratio_spherex_over_jwst|f2]] ± [[e11.targets.wise0855.jwst.comparison.PRISM.mean_ratio_err|f2]] times the JWST NIRSpec spectrum (Luhman et al. 2024). The two reddest steps, at 4.8–5.0 µm, fall below it. Passing the JWST spectrum through each SPHEREx picture's own spectral response, rather than averaging it in 0.1 µm steps, gives the same ratio, [[e32s.r_new|f2]] ± [[e32s.r_new_err|f2]], and the two reddest steps stay low. A SPHEREx spectrum of WISE 0855 has been published (Rustamkulov et al. 2026); ours is an independent comparison with JWST.

[Methods, section 4](methods.html#stacking)

![SPHEREx spectrum of WISE 0855−0714 next to JWST](figs/v2/e11_wise0855.svg "SPHEREx spectrum in 0.1 µm steps (circles; open circles are below 3σ; bars are 1σ), the JWST NIRSpec spectrum (thin line) averaged over the same steps (squares), and the Spitzer 4.5 µm measurement (diamond).")

## Distance to Epsilon Indi B {#e12}

Epsilon Indi B is a pair of brown dwarfs that SPHEREx sees as one source. Fitting its SPHEREx positions over a year for position, proper motion and parallax gives a parallax of [[e12.parallax_mas|f0]] ± [[e12.parallax_err_mas.adopted|f0]] mas, a distance of [[e12.distance_ly|f1]] light-years (68% interval [[e12.distance_interval_ly_68.0|f1]]–[[e12.distance_interval_ly_68.1|f1]]). The archive's astrometry is tied to Gaia DR3 reference stars, but no Gaia measurement of Epsilon Indi B itself is used; Gaia measures [[e12.gaia_parallax_mas|f1]] mas. Ordinary stars in the same pictures show a small shared offset that mimics a parallax of [[e12.null.summary.mean_mas|f0]] mas; it is not subtracted, and the quoted uncertainty is the root-mean-square of those [[e12.null.summary.n_used]] stars' parallaxes about zero, which includes their shared offset.

[Methods, section 5](methods.html#astrometry)

![Epsilon Indi B's path and parallax](figs/v2/e12_parallax.svg "a, positions over a year: single pictures (small points), visit averages (circles), and fits with proper motion only (dotted) and with parallax (dash-dot). b, visit averages with the proper motion removed, and the fitted parallax ellipse. Bars are the standard error of each visit mean.")

## Brown dwarfs classified by methane {#e15}

Methane absorption at 3.3 µm distinguishes T and Y dwarfs from L dwarfs. An index formed from the flux in the 3.2–3.4 µm window relative to the flux on either side classifies [[e15.calibration.loo_accuracy|pct]] of [[e15.calibration.n_decisive]] brown dwarfs with published spectral types correctly when each is left out of the calibration in turn.

[Methods, section 6](methods.html#spectra)

![Methane index against colour](figs/v2/e15_methane.svg "The 4.4–5.0 µm to 1.0–1.3 µm flux ratio against the methane index, for brown dwarfs of known spectral type and for candidates without a published spectrum. The dashed line is the dividing value; bars are 1σ.")

## Cold brown dwarfs with methane measured {#methane-candidates}

We applied the same methane test, unchanged, to [[e22s.n_measured]] objects that earlier surveys listed as possible brown dwarfs. [[e22s.n_decisive]] were bright enough for a clear answer: [[e22s.n_T_or_later]] show the methane dip of cold brown dwarfs (T type or later, below roughly 1300 K), and [[e22s.n_L_like]] do not. None of [[e22s.control_n]] known warmer L dwarfs measured the same way was wrongly given the dip. A second check of the literature and of neighbouring sources removed one with a published methane-imaging type and [[deriveds.e22_vet_doubtful]] with a brighter star within one to two SPHEREx pixels, leaving [[deriveds.e22_vet_keep]] with no published spectral type. Their colours already suggested T dwarfs; SPHEREx now measures their methane directly. Each still needs a spectrum to confirm it.

