## Variable stars {#e18}

Tested on real stars across the sky, SPHEREx alone recovers [[e10.recall.testable_variables.k]] of [[e10.recall.testable_variables.n]] catalogued variable stars that it can test (long-period variables: [[e10.recall.by_type.LPV.testable.k]] of [[e10.recall.by_type.LPV.testable.n]]), while flagging [[e10.false_positive.test_half_T.k]] of [[e10.false_positive.test_half_T.n]] steady comparison stars. R Draconis peaked [[e28s.rdra_D6_offset_from_prediction_d|f1]] days from the date predicted from earlier pictures.

SPHEREx observes the north ecliptic pole on almost every orbit. Within 0.4° of the pole, [[e18a.data.n_reported]] catalogued sources were measured in [[e18bs.n_pictures|int]] pictures. The measurements of all stars share small errors: calibration offsets that differ from picture to picture, a dependence on position on the detector, and slow drifts over the year. We fit a correction for these to half of the non-variable stars and test it on the other half. Variations planted into real light curves set the depth: with the correction, half are recovered at an amplitude of [[e26s.floor_after_12_16_pct|int]]% for sources of AB magnitude 12–16, [[e26s.floor_after_16_17_pct|int]]% at 16–17 and [[e26s.floor_after_17_19_pct|int]]% at 17–19, at a false-alarm rate of [[e26s.fa_after_rate|pct]]. Most catalogued variables in the field vary by less than this; [[e26.detail.after.classes.catalogued.n_above]] of [[e26.detail.after.classes.catalogued.n]] pass the threshold.

